WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.795
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.795 --> 00:00:06.323
In this lesson we explore the Power Query Editor, where we clean and prepare

3
00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:07.903
data in Power BI.

4
00:00:07.903 --> 00:00:11.062
Here we have loaded the Financial Sample workbook.

5
00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:16.591
In Query Settings on the right, I rename the query to Financial Sample, so

6
00:00:16.591 --> 00:00:18.960
it is easy to find later.

7
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.042
When we close and apply, the queries appear in the Data pane of the

8
00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:25.380
report, ready to use.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.600
Back in the Power Query Editor, the Queries pane on the left lists every

10
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.800
table we loaded.

11
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.600
Let's open the Financial Sample query again.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.266
Under each column header there is a thin bar.

13
00:00:53.266 --> 00:00:55.807
Hover over it to see column quality:

14
00:00:55.807 --> 00:01:00.888
the share of values that are valid, that have errors, and that are empty.

15
00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:04.880
Here every value is valid, with no errors and nothing empty.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.795
On the right, Applied Steps records each change in order:

17
00:01:11.795 --> 00:01:15.210
the source, the navigation, promoted headers, and changed type.

18
00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:20.523
You can click any step to see the data at that point, or remove

19
00:01:20.523 --> 00:01:22.800
a step you do not need.

20
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.236
Before we clean anything, it pays to understand the data.

21
00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:48.895
On the View tab, we can switch on three helpful options:

22
00:01:48.895 --> 00:01:51.860
column quality, column distribution, and column profile.

23
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.993
Column distribution adds a small chart under each header.

24
00:02:04.993 --> 00:02:09.874
It shows how many distinct and unique values each column has.

25
00:02:09.874 --> 00:02:13.423
Segment, for example, has only five distinct values.

26
00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:17.860
From here you can also remove duplicates in one click.

27
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.136
Column profile shows full statistics for the selected column at the bottom of the screen:

28
00:02:32.136 --> 00:02:38.682
the count, errors, empty values, distinct values, and a chart of how often each value appears.

29
00:02:38.682 --> 00:02:44.000
This is a quick way to spot problems before you build your report.

